---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TRACKS, topicsInTrack } from '../data/topics';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base title="spring-framework-learnings" current="tracks">
  <main class="shell">
    <article>
      <h1>How Spring actually behaves</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        It is very easy to write a confident, plausible, wrong explanation of a Spring internal. So
        every topic here gets two things: a note written after reading the real source, and a lab
        module whose tests prove what the note claims. <code>mvn test</code> is the fact-checker.
      </p>

      <div class="tracks">
        {
          TRACKS.map((track) => (
            <section class="track">
              <h2>{track.title}</h2>
              <p class="blurb">{track.blurb}</p>
              {topicsInTrack(track.id).map((topic) => (
                <div class={`topic ${topic.status}`}>
                  <span class={`dot ${topic.status}`} />
                  <span class="name">
                    {topic.status === 'shipped' ? (
                      <a href={`${base}/notes/${topic.slug}`}>{topic.title}</a>
                    ) : (
                      topic.title
                    )}
                  </span>
                  {topic.tests && <span class="tests">{topic.tests} tests</span>}
                </div>
              ))}
            </section>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h2 id="method">How these notes are written</h2>
      <p>
        The order matters, because writing the prose first produces notes that sound right and are
        not. Read the implementation. Write a test that would fail if the claim were wrong in either
        direction. Only then write the explanation, with a pointer to the test underneath it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to do the reading yourself, start with
        <a href={`${base}/notes/reading-the-source`}>how to read Spring source</a>, then follow a
        <a href={`${base}/paths`}>code path</a> — an ordered list of the classes a feature actually
        goes through, with every class and method checked against the real Spring jars by a test.
      </p>

      <h2 id="version">Version under study</h2>
      <p>
        Spring Framework 7.0.9 and Spring Boot 4.1.1, on Java 21. Version-specific behaviour is
        called out rather than smoothed over, because that is usually the part that bites on an
        upgrade.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
